WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
Dependency security starts with knowing what is in the product.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:15.960
A useful inventory includes direct dependencies, important transitive dependencies, versions, source locations, build options, license obligations, deployment targets, and the reason each dependency exists.

3
00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:23.260
Without that record, a team may not know which applications are affected when an advisory appears or when a library reaches end of support.

4
00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.740
Ownership is just as important as the list.

5
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.240
Every meaningful dependency should have an engineering owner or owning team.

6
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:45.500
That owner does not need to make every update alone, but someone must watch release notes, evaluate advisories, understand build configuration, approve feature changes, and remove the dependency when it is no longer needed.

7
00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.640
Dependencies with no owner tend to become stale and surprising.

8
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:54.280
SBOM-style records can improve visibility.

9
00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:01.900
They help security teams, product teams, release teams, and customers understand what components are present.

10
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.280
They also make incident response more precise.

11
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:15.380
When a dependency issue is announced, the team can ask whether the affected version is present, whether the affected feature is enabled, and which shipped artifacts need attention.

12
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.220
Inventory should also drive reduction.

13
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:25.920
Unused libraries, duplicate implementations, old compatibility packages, and forgotten sample tools add maintenance burden.

14
00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:33.940
Removing a dependency can be one of the strongest security improvements because it eliminates update work, configuration uncertainty, and future exposure.

15
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:39.960
A clean inventory keeps dependency decisions visible, assigned, and connected to product value.
